# Congratulations on setting up {{ site_title }}

This is your site to make into whatever you want. This file gives you a little bit of
direction for your next steps.

## Install the dependencies

With [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/), which reads the dependencies from `pyproject.toml`:

```shell
uv sync
```

With pip, create a virtual environment and install the packages:

```shell
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
pip install render-engine render-engine-cli
```

On Windows, activate the virtual environment with `.venv\Scripts\activate` instead.

The commands below use `uv run`. If you're using pip, run them without the `uv run` prefix,
with your virtual environment activated.

## Build and preview your site

There are two ways to work with your site. Both produce the same result in `{{ output_path }}/`.

### With the Render Engine CLI (recommended)

The [Render Engine CLI](https://github.com/render-engine/render-engine-cli) gives you a few
handy commands. If it isn't installed yet, add it with `uv add --dev render-engine-cli`
or `pip install render-engine-cli`.

Build your site and serve it at `http://localhost:8000` (the console shows the link):

```shell
uv run render-engine serve --reload
```

With `--reload`, the site is rebuilt every time you change something in `content/` or
`templates/`. Add `--clean` to remove the previous output before building.

To create a new page or blog post, run:

```shell
uv run render-engine new-entry --filename my-new-post.md --title "My new post" --include-date
```

This creates the content file in your default collection and opens it in your editor.
`--include-date` adds today's date, which blog posts need for sorting.
Use `--collection` to pick a different collection.

To only build the site, without serving it:

```shell
uv run render-engine build
```

### Without the CLI

**Build** the site by running your app file. It calls `app.render()`, which writes
the finished site into `{{ output_path }}/`:

```shell
uv run python {{ app_filename }}
```

**Preview** it by serving the output folder, then open `http://localhost:8000` in your browser:

```shell
uv run python -m http.server --bind 127.0.0.1 -d {{ output_path }}
```

There's no automatic rebuilding here: run the build command again after each change.
New pages and blog posts are created by hand as files in `content/`.

## Check the configuration

We've set up a `pyproject.toml` for you with:

- your project's dependencies
- the configuration the CLI uses to find your site, under `[tool.render-engine.cli]`:
  - `module`: the file that defines your site (`{{ app_filename }}`)
  - `site`: the name of the `Site` object in that file (`app`)
  - `collection`: the default collection for `new-entry`, if you included a blog or a collection

If you rename `{{ app_filename }}` or the objects inside it, update `pyproject.toml` to match.

## Project layout

- `{{ app_filename }}`: your site's settings, pages and collections
- `content/`: the content of your pages and blog posts
- `templates/`: how your pages look
- `static/`: CSS, images and other files copied as they are

